import {makeCards} from './helpers';
const g=(leaf,rows)=>makeCards(['Microeconomics',leaf],rows);

export const microeconomics=[
...g('Consumer Theory',[
['Preference Relations','What has to be true before preferences can be represented consistently by a utility function?','Standard consumer theory assumes a preference relation that can compare feasible bundles and is sufficiently consistent, typically complete and transitive. Utility then represents the ranking; the numbers themselves need not have intrinsic psychological meaning.'],
['Completeness','What does completeness require of a consumer’s preferences?','For any two feasible bundles, the consumer can rank one above the other or regard them as indifferent. Completeness rules out unresolved comparisons. It is an analytical convenience and can fail when choices are unfamiliar or multidimensional.'],
['Transitivity','Why is transitivity important for coherent choice?','If A is preferred to B and B to C, transitivity requires A to be preferred to C. Violations can produce cycles that make optimization ill-defined and potentially allow a sequence of trades that leaves the consumer worse off.'],
['Utility Representation','Why is utility usually ordinal rather than cardinal in modern microeconomics?','Only the ranking of bundles matters for choice. Any increasing transformation of a utility function represents the same preferences. Saying one bundle has utility 20 and another 10 therefore does not normally mean it delivers twice as much welfare.'],
['Indifference Curves','What does the slope of an indifference curve encode?','It reflects the marginal rate of substitution: how much of one good the consumer is willing to give up for another while holding utility constant. Curvature captures willingness to substitute across different bundle compositions.'],
['Marginal Rate of Substitution','Why does an interior consumer optimum equate the MRS to the relative price?','If willingness to trade goods differs from the market tradeoff, the consumer can reallocate spending to become better off. At an interior optimum, the subjective tradeoff and budget tradeoff are aligned.'],
['Budget Constraint','Why is the budget constraint as important as preferences in determining demand?','Preferences describe what the consumer wants; prices and income define what is feasible. A price change rotates the budget set and changes both relative incentives and purchasing power, producing substitution and income effects.'],
['Consumer Optimization','What does the standard consumer problem actually solve?','The consumer chooses the most preferred feasible bundle given prices and income. Interior solutions satisfy tangency conditions, while corner solutions arise when the optimum lies at a boundary and marginal conditions need not equalize.'],
['Corner Solutions','Why can a consumer optimum occur without equal marginal utility per euro across goods?','If one good is unattractive enough at prevailing prices, the consumer may choose zero of it. The usual tangency condition then does not apply because the non-negativity constraint binds.'],
['Marshallian Demand','What does Marshallian demand hold fixed when price changes?','It describes utility-maximizing demand as a function of market prices and nominal income. Because real purchasing power changes with price, Marshallian demand combines substitution and income effects.'],
['Hicksian Demand','Why is Hicksian demand useful even though consumers do not literally receive utility compensation?','It asks how much of each good would minimize expenditure while maintaining a fixed utility level. Holding utility constant isolates pure substitution and supports exact welfare measures such as compensating variation.'],
['Expenditure Function','What information does the expenditure function summarize?','For any price vector and target utility, it gives the minimum expenditure needed to reach that utility. Its derivatives yield Hicksian demands under regular conditions, linking welfare and demand theory.'],
['Indirect Utility','Why is an indirect utility function useful for comparative statics?','It gives the maximum utility attainable at given prices and income. This lets analysts study how market conditions affect welfare without resolving the entire bundle choice every time.'],
['Slutsky Equation','What does the Slutsky equation separate inside a price response?','It decomposes the total change in Marshallian demand into a compensated substitution effect and an income effect caused by the change in real purchasing power. This is central to interpreting ordinary demand curves.'],
['Substitution Effect','Why must the compensated own-price substitution effect be nonpositive?','Holding utility constant, a good that becomes relatively more expensive cannot be substituted toward under standard preferences. This result is stronger than the sign of uncompensated demand because income effects can offset it.'],
['Income Effect','Why can an income effect reverse the ordinary law of demand?','A price increase reduces real purchasing power. For an inferior good, that income effect raises desired consumption and can theoretically outweigh the substitution effect, producing a Giffen good.'],
['Normal Goods','What makes a good normal?','Demand rises when income rises, holding prices constant. Normality is an empirical property over a range of income, not a moral or qualitative label; the same good can change category across consumers or income levels.'],
['Inferior Goods','Why can demand fall when income rises?','As households become richer they may substitute away from low-cost goods toward preferred alternatives. Inferiority concerns the income effect and does not imply poor quality in any universal sense.'],
['Giffen Goods','What extreme combination of effects is required for a Giffen good?','The good must be strongly inferior and occupy enough of the budget that the negative income effect from a price increase outweighs the ordinary substitution effect. The phenomenon is theoretically important but empirically rare.'],
['Compensating Variation','How much money would make a consumer whole after a price change?','Compensating variation measures the income adjustment required after the price change to restore the original utility. It is an exact money-metric welfare measure based on Hicksian demand.'],
['Equivalent Variation','How does equivalent variation differ from compensating variation?','Equivalent variation asks how much income at original prices would generate the same utility change as the proposed price movement. The two measures differ when income effects are significant.'],
['Revealed Preference','Can preference restrictions be tested without measuring utility directly?','Observed choices reveal that selected bundles were preferred to affordable alternatives. Consistency conditions such as WARP or GARP can therefore test whether behavior is compatible with utility maximization.'],
['WARP','What does the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference rule out?','If a consumer chooses A when B was affordable, the consumer should not later choose B when A is affordable under conditions implying strict revealed preference. WARP excludes simple choice reversals inconsistent with stable preferences.'],
['Intertemporal Choice','How is saving a relative-price decision across dates?','The real interest rate determines how much future consumption can be obtained by giving up current consumption. Households choose a path balancing time preference, expected income, risk and borrowing constraints.'],
['Consumption Smoothing','Why can consumption move less than current income?','Households can save during temporarily high income and borrow or dissave during temporarily low income. Permanent-income expectations, uncertainty and access to credit determine how closely consumption follows current earnings.'],
['Borrowing Constraints','How do borrowing constraints change the standard Euler-equation prediction?','A constrained household cannot freely transfer future resources into the present. Current consumption then responds strongly to cash-on-hand even if expected lifetime income has not changed.'],
['Expected Utility','How does expected utility represent choice under known risk?','The decision-maker evaluates each outcome through utility and weights it by probability. Risk attitudes come from curvature of utility over wealth or consumption. Prospect theory modifies both outcome evaluation and probability weighting.'],
['Risk Aversion','What does concave utility imply about a fair gamble?','Because utility rises at a decreasing rate, the utility loss from a downside exceeds the gain from an equally sized upside around the same wealth level. The agent therefore prefers the certain expected value to a fair risky prospect.'],
['Certainty Equivalent','What does a certainty equivalent reveal about risk preferences?','It is the sure amount that makes the decision-maker indifferent to a gamble. For a risk-averse person it lies below the gamble’s expected monetary value; the difference is the risk premium.'],
['Insurance Demand','Why does risk aversion create willingness to pay more than expected loss for insurance?','Insurance trades a certain premium for removal of a volatile loss. Concave utility makes smoothing wealth across states valuable, so actuarially fair or moderately loaded insurance can raise expected utility.']
]),
...g('Producer Theory',[
['Production Function','What information does a production function encode?','It maps feasible input combinations into maximum output under a given technology. It is a technological relation, not a behavioral rule; cost minimization and profit maximization determine which point the firm actually chooses.'],
['Cobb–Douglas Production','Why is the Cobb–Douglas form used so often?','It is tractable, has simple elasticities and can represent constant, increasing or decreasing returns to scale depending on exponents. Its convenience should not be confused with universal empirical accuracy.'],
['CES Production','What does the CES function add beyond Cobb–Douglas?','Constant-elasticity-of-substitution technology allows the ease of substituting capital for labor to differ from one. That elasticity matters greatly for automation, distribution and the response of factor shares to relative prices.'],
['Marginal Product','Why can the marginal product differ from average productivity?','Marginal product is the extra output from one more unit of an input, holding others fixed; average product divides total output by the amount used. Hiring and input-demand decisions depend on the margin, not the historical average.'],
['Diminishing Marginal Returns','Why is diminishing marginal return a short-run concept?','It arises when one input increases while at least one other productive input is fixed. It is distinct from decreasing returns to scale, which concerns proportional expansion of all inputs.'],
['Isoquants','What does an isoquant represent?','It shows input combinations capable of producing the same output. Its slope is the marginal rate of technical substitution, revealing how technology permits one input to replace another locally.'],
['Marginal Rate of Technical Substitution','Why does cost minimization equate the MRTS to the input-price ratio?','If the technological tradeoff differs from the market cost tradeoff, the firm can substitute toward the input that delivers output more cheaply. At an interior cost-minimizing bundle the two tradeoffs coincide.'],
['Fixed Costs','Why do fixed costs matter for entry but not always for short-run output?','A sunk or unavoidable fixed cost does not change marginal cost once the firm is operating, so it need not affect the optimal short-run quantity. But expected fixed costs strongly affect whether entering or remaining in the market is profitable.'],
['Variable Costs','What makes a cost variable?','It changes with the chosen level of output or input use. Variable cost shapes short-run marginal decisions and determines the shutdown condition when revenue no longer covers avoidable operating cost.'],
['Marginal Cost','Why is marginal cost central to efficient output decisions?','It measures the opportunity cost of producing one additional unit. A competitive firm expands until price equals marginal cost; a social planner compares social marginal benefit with social marginal cost.'],
['Average Cost','Why can average cost fall while marginal cost is rising?','As long as marginal cost remains below average cost, the next unit pulls the average down. Average cost reaches its minimum where marginal cost crosses it from below.'],
['Short-Run Cost','What distinguishes short-run cost from long-run cost?','At least one productive factor or capacity choice is fixed in the short run. Long-run cost allows all inputs and plant size to adjust, so the firm can move to a different technology or capacity scale.'],
['Long-Run Cost','Why is the long-run average-cost curve an envelope of short-run possibilities?','For each output level the firm chooses the plant or input configuration with the lowest attainable cost. Short-run curves describe operating around a fixed capacity; the long-run curve selects across capacities.'],
['Cost Minimization','Why can cost minimization be analyzed separately from profit maximization?','For any target output, a profit-maximizing firm never wants to produce it using an unnecessarily expensive input mix. Solving the conditional cost problem first separates technology from the later decision about how much output to sell.'],
['Conditional Factor Demand','What determines the cost-minimizing demand for an input at a given output?','Input prices and technology determine the combination that produces the target output at minimum cost. These conditional demands do not yet account for the firm’s decision about the optimal scale of production.'],
['Returns to Scale','What does constant returns to scale mean precisely?','If all inputs are multiplied by the same factor, output changes by that factor. Increasing or decreasing returns refer to more-than-proportional or less-than-proportional output responses to scaling every input together.'],
['Economies of Scale','Why are economies of scale not identical to increasing returns to scale?','Returns to scale is a technological property; economies of scale concern how average cost changes with output and can reflect input prices or fixed costs as well. They often move together but are conceptually distinct.'],
['Diseconomies of Scale','Why might average cost rise in very large firms?','Coordination, bureaucracy, communication and managerial complexity can grow faster than productive scale benefits. Diseconomies need not be technological in a narrow engineering sense; organization itself can create them.'],
['Economies of Scope','When is joint production cheaper than separate production?','Scope economies exist when producing multiple goods together uses shared inputs, distribution, data, branding or knowledge more efficiently than separate firms could. They help explain multiproduct firms and platform ecosystems.'],
['Learning by Doing','How can production today reduce future cost?','Repeated activity generates process knowledge, worker skill and organizational improvements. The learning curve makes cumulative output a state variable, creating dynamic advantages for early producers.'],
['Total Factor Productivity','Why is TFP not a direct measure of technology?','It is the portion of output not explained by measured inputs under a chosen production model. Management, utilization, reallocation, measurement error and technology all enter the residual.'],
['Profit Maximization','What is the marginal condition for a profit-maximizing firm with market power?','It produces where marginal revenue equals marginal cost, provided operating is profitable. A competitive firm is the special case where marginal revenue equals market price.'],
['Input Demand','Why is demand for labor or capital called derived demand?','Inputs are valuable because they contribute to producing output that can be sold. Their demand therefore depends on output prices, technology, other input prices and the marginal revenue product they generate.'],
['Shutdown Condition','Why can a firm keep operating while making an accounting loss?','In the short run, fixed costs may be unavoidable. Operating is worthwhile if revenue covers variable cost and contributes something toward fixed obligations. The shutdown threshold therefore differs from the long-run exit condition.'],
['Entry and Exit','How does entry drive long-run economic profit toward zero in a competitive industry?','Positive profit attracts entrants, increasing supply and reducing price; persistent losses induce exit. Zero economic profit still includes the opportunity cost of capital and entrepreneurial effort, so it is not the same as zero accounting earnings.'],
['Envelope Theorem','Why can an optimized value change without tracking every adjustment in the decision variables?','At an optimum, first-order changes through the chosen controls vanish locally. The derivative of the value function with respect to a parameter can therefore be computed from its direct effect, simplifying comparative statics.'],
["Shephard's Lemma",'How can a cost function reveal conditional input demands?','Differentiating the minimized cost function with respect to an input price gives the firm’s cost-minimizing demand for that input under regularity conditions. The result connects dual cost analysis to observable input behavior.'],
["Hotelling's Lemma",'How can the profit function recover supply and factor demand?','Differentiating maximized profit with respect to an output price yields supply; differentiating with respect to an input price yields negative input demand. This duality is central to producer theory and empirical work.']
]),
...g('Markets, Equilibrium & Welfare',[
['Supply','When does a competitive supply curve coincide with marginal cost?','For a price-taking firm in the short run, the supply curve is the portion of marginal cost above the shutdown threshold. With market power there is generally no supply curve independent of demand because the firm chooses price and quantity strategically.'],
['Demand','Why can observed price and quantity movements fail to trace a demand curve?','Both demand and supply move over time. Equilibrium observations mix the two. Estimating demand requires a source of variation that shifts supply without directly shifting demand, or another credible identification strategy.'],
['Competitive Equilibrium','What does a competitive equilibrium guarantee and what does it not?','It makes individual plans mutually consistent at prices each agent treats as given. Efficiency requires additional assumptions; fairness is not implied at all. Equilibrium is a consistency condition, not a moral verdict.'],
['Comparative Statics','What does comparative statics leave out?','It compares equilibrium before and after a parameter change without modeling the transition path. This is powerful for direction and incidence but can miss adjustment speed, temporary overshooting and dynamic constraints.'],
['Price Elasticity of Demand','Why does elasticity govern a monopolist’s feasible markup?','When demand is very elastic, a price increase loses many customers, limiting markup. The inverse-elasticity condition formalizes the relationship between market power and responsiveness under simple monopoly pricing.'],
['Price Elasticity of Supply','Why is supply usually more elastic over longer horizons?','Firms gain time to expand capacity, adopt technology, enter or exit. Short-run physical constraints can therefore make quantity almost fixed even when long-run supply responds strongly.'],
['Cross-Price Elasticity','How can cross-price elasticity reveal whether goods are substitutes or complements?','Demand for a substitute tends to rise when the other good becomes more expensive; demand for a complement tends to fall. The magnitude also helps define competitive market boundaries empirically.'],
['Income Elasticity','Why does income elasticity matter for structural change?','As income rises, expenditure shares move differently across necessities, luxuries and inferior goods. These patterns help explain why agriculture’s share tends to fall and services expand during development.'],
['Consumer Surplus','When is the area under demand a good welfare approximation?','For small price changes or limited income effects, willingness-to-pay demand provides a useful money measure of consumer gains. Large changes call for Hicksian measures that hold utility consistently.'],
['Producer Surplus','Who ultimately receives producer surplus?','It can accrue to owners, workers, landowners or suppliers depending on contracts and factor-market adjustment. The area above supply is a market-level measure, not a direct map to one social group.'],
['Deadweight Loss','Why does a distortion create deadweight loss rather than merely transfer surplus?','A wedge prevents trades whose social marginal benefit exceeds social marginal cost. Transfers such as tax revenue can be redistributed; the lost mutually beneficial transactions are the efficiency cost.'],
['Tax Incidence','Why is statutory incidence irrelevant to the long-run economic burden?','Prices and quantities adjust until behavioral responses equilibrate the market. The less elastic side tends to bear more of the burden because it has fewer alternatives. General-equilibrium and mobility effects can shift incidence further.'],
['Subsidy Incidence','Who captures a subsidy paid to buyers?','Legal receipt does not determine economic benefit. If supply is inelastic, sellers can capture much of the subsidy through higher prices; if demand is inelastic, buyers retain more. The same elasticity logic as taxation applies.'],
['Price Ceilings','Why can a binding price ceiling change quality as well as quantity?','When price cannot clear the market, sellers can ration through waiting, screening, side payments or reduced quality. The welfare effect depends on both the shortage and how scarce units are allocated.'],
['Price Floors','Why can the same price floor have opposite employment effects under competition and monopsony?','A competitive floor above equilibrium reduces quantity demanded. A monopsonist already restricts employment to push wages down, so a moderate minimum wage can raise both wage and employment before eventually becoming too high.'],
['Quota Rents','Who gains when a quantity restriction raises market price?','The right to sell or import the restricted quantity becomes valuable. If permits are auctioned, the government captures the rent; if allocated freely, license holders do. The distribution can differ greatly from an equivalent tax.'],
['Pareto Efficiency','Why is Pareto efficiency a deliberately weak welfare criterion?','It identifies changes that help someone without hurting anyone but cannot rank most real policies, which create winners and losers. It avoids interpersonal utility comparisons at the cost of limited decisiveness.'],
['Kaldor–Hicks Criterion','What does potential compensation add to welfare analysis?','A change passes if winners gain enough that they could compensate losers and still remain better off. Compensation need not actually occur, so the criterion separates aggregate surplus from distribution and can conflict with equity concerns.'],
['First Welfare Theorem','Why is the first welfare theorem best viewed as a diagnostic benchmark?','It tells us what conditions make competitive equilibrium efficient. When outcomes look inefficient, analysts can search for the assumption that fails: market power, externality, information problem, missing market or nonconvexity.'],
['Second Welfare Theorem','Why does redistribution become difficult outside the theorem’s idealized setting?','The theorem relies on lump-sum transfers that change resources without changing marginal incentives. Real taxes and transfers depend on behavior and information, so redistribution usually alters efficiency as well as endowments.'],
['General Equilibrium','What feedback does partial equilibrium miss?','Price changes alter income, factor demand and spending elsewhere. A broad tax, trade shock or energy disruption can therefore have system-wide effects that cannot be captured by holding all other markets fixed.'],
['Edgeworth Box','Why is the contract curve not a unique social optimum?','It contains every Pareto-efficient allocation between two consumers. Which point society selects depends on initial endowments, bargaining or distributional criteria that efficiency alone cannot resolve.'],
['Contract Curve','What condition characterizes interior points on the exchange contract curve?','Consumers’ marginal rates of substitution are equal, so no mutually beneficial reallocation remains. Boundary points can also be efficient when constraints bind.'],
['Social Welfare Function','What extra assumption does a social welfare function introduce?','It aggregates individual welfare using explicit or implicit weights. This moves beyond Pareto analysis and makes distributional judgments unavoidable rather than hiding them inside an efficiency criterion.'],
['Shadow Price','Why can a resource have a high economic value even if its accounting price is zero?','If a constraint is binding, one additional unit can relax it and increase the objective. The Lagrange multiplier measures that marginal value. Congested infrastructure and emissions caps are common examples.'],
['Market Clearing','Does market clearing mean everyone gets what they want?','No. It means quantity supplied equals quantity demanded at prevailing prices given budgets and constraints. Individuals can still be poor, rationed by income or dissatisfied. Clearing is a consistency condition.'],
['Excess Demand','Why can excess demand drive price adjustment in some models but not others?','Walrasian tatonnement assumes prices respond to aggregate excess demand before trade occurs. Real markets use inventories, queues, bargaining and posted prices, so adjustment mechanisms differ by institution.'],
['Walras Law','What does Walras law imply about excess demand across markets?','The value of aggregate excess demand sums to zero when all budget constraints hold. If all but one markets clear, the final market clears automatically in value terms. The result is accounting consistency, not a dynamic stability theorem.']
]),
...g('Industrial Organization',[
['Perfect Competition','Why is perfect competition useful even when few industries literally satisfy it?','It provides a benchmark with price-taking, free entry and no strategic interaction. Deviations—markups, barriers, information frictions—can then be studied explicitly. The benchmark is diagnostic rather than descriptive.'],
['Monopoly','Why does a monopolist restrict output relative to a competitive benchmark?','The monopolist recognizes that selling more requires lowering price on marginal units, so marginal revenue lies below price. Profit maximization at MR = MC therefore produces a markup and lower quantity than price-taking supply would.'],
['Lerner Index','How does the Lerner index connect markup to demand elasticity?','The index (P−MC)/P measures price-cost margin. Under simple monopoly pricing it equals the inverse absolute elasticity of demand. High markups require demand that is locally less responsive or barriers that limit substitution.'],
['Price Discrimination','Why can charging different prices increase output rather than simply redistribute surplus?','If a firm can segment customers by willingness to pay, it may profitably serve low-value buyers at lower prices while charging high-value buyers more. Some forms therefore expand quantity even as they transfer surplus toward the seller.'],
['First-Degree Price Discrimination','What happens to deadweight loss under perfect personalized pricing?','If the monopolist can charge each buyer exactly their willingness to pay, efficient quantity can be produced while the seller captures nearly all consumer surplus. Efficiency improves but distribution shifts dramatically.'],
['Second-Degree Price Discrimination','Why do quantity discounts or product versions reveal hidden willingness to pay?','The seller offers a menu designed so different customer types self-select. Contract features are deliberately distorted to satisfy incentive compatibility, turning product design into a screening mechanism.'],
['Third-Degree Price Discrimination','When can segment-specific pricing raise or lower welfare?','The firm sets different prices across identifiable groups with different elasticities. Welfare depends on whether discrimination mainly redistributes existing sales or expands output into markets that would otherwise be unserved.'],
['Two-Part Tariff','Why combine a fixed fee with a per-unit price?','The per-unit charge can be set near marginal cost to encourage efficient usage while the fixed fee extracts part of the resulting surplus. Membership clubs and telecom contracts often use this logic.'],
['Bundling','Why can selling products together raise profit?','If willingness to pay is imperfectly correlated across products, bundling can reduce heterogeneity in total valuation and improve surplus extraction. Bundles can also exploit complementarities or create exclusion concerns.'],
['Oligopoly','Why does strategic interaction make oligopoly fundamentally different from monopoly or competition?','Each firm’s optimal action depends on rivals’ responses. Quantity, price, capacity, product differentiation and timing therefore change equilibrium. There is no single oligopoly outcome without specifying the game.'],
['Cournot Competition','What strategic variable do firms choose in the Cournot model?','Firms choose quantities simultaneously while anticipating that total quantity determines market price. More firms typically push output toward the competitive level, but each retains some market power.'],
['Bertrand Competition','Why can two firms be enough to produce the competitive price in the simplest Bertrand model?','With identical products and no capacity constraints, either firm can capture the entire market by slightly undercutting its rival. Price competition therefore drives price to marginal cost. Product differentiation or capacity constraints overturn the result.'],
['Stackelberg Competition','What advantage does a quantity leader obtain?','The leader commits to output first and anticipates the follower’s best response. Strategic commitment lets it capture a larger market share and profit than in simultaneous Cournot competition under standard assumptions.'],
['Product Differentiation','How can differentiation soften price competition without creating a legal monopoly?','When products are imperfect substitutes, raising price does not lose every customer. Firms acquire downward-sloping residual demand and can sustain markups. Differentiation can reflect genuine variety as well as strategic insulation.'],
['Hotelling Model','Why can firms locate close together even when consumers would prefer more variety?','Spatial or characteristic competition creates incentives to capture marginal customers near the center. The exact result depends on pricing and transport-cost assumptions, but the model illustrates endogenous differentiation.'],
['Entry Deterrence','When can an incumbent profitably sacrifice current profit to discourage entry?','Capacity investment, limit pricing, exclusive contracts or product proliferation can change an entrant’s expected post-entry payoff. A deterrence strategy must be credible and cheaper than accommodating entry.'],
['Limit Pricing','Why is a low pre-entry price not always a credible deterrent?','If the incumbent would raise price after entry regardless of its earlier behavior, the entrant should ignore the threat. Limit pricing works only when price conveys private information about cost or is tied to an irreversible strategic commitment.'],
['Predatory Pricing','Why is pricing below cost difficult to distinguish from aggressive competition?','Low prices benefit consumers immediately and can reflect efficiency. A predation theory requires a plausible mechanism for driving rivals out and later recouping losses through market power.'],
['Vertical Integration','How can integration both improve efficiency and threaten competition?','It can eliminate double marginalization, protect specific investments and improve coordination. It can also foreclose rivals from essential inputs or customers. Assessment therefore depends on the transaction and market structure.'],
['Double Marginalization','Why can two monopolists in a vertical chain charge a higher final price than one integrated monopolist?','Each layer adds its own markup without internalizing the harm imposed on the other. Integration or appropriately designed contracts can coordinate pricing and reduce the combined distortion.'],
['Vertical Restraints','Why can resale-price maintenance be either harmful or efficiency-enhancing?','It can facilitate collusion or soften price competition, but it can also induce retailers to provide service or prevent free-riding on promotion. Economic analysis must identify the mechanism rather than infer effect from the form alone.'],
['Network Effects','Why can network effects create both value and entry barriers?','A larger user base increases the value of joining, which can generate rapid tipping and lock-in. The same feedback that creates coordination benefits makes it harder for a superior entrant to attract users from an established network.'],
['Two-Sided Markets','Why might a platform rationally price one side below cost?','Demand on each side depends on participation on the other. Subsidizing users can attract sellers, advertisers or developers whose participation generates revenue elsewhere. Price structure matters as much as the total price level.'],
['Platform Economics','What makes ordinary one-market markup logic unreliable for platforms?','Cross-side network effects mean changing a price on one side alters demand and surplus on another. Market definition, incidence and competition policy must therefore account for the whole ecosystem.'],
['Switching Costs','How can switching costs create market power after competition for the customer has occurred?','Firms may compete aggressively to acquire customers, then charge more once users face learning, contractual, data or compatibility costs. Dynamic competition can therefore differ sharply from current observed prices.'],
['Lock-In','When does path dependence become an entry barrier?','If users have sunk complementary investments or their contacts use the incumbent standard, moving individually is costly even when everyone might prefer a coordinated switch. Interoperability can reduce this coordination barrier.'],
['Contestable Markets','Why can potential entry discipline an incumbent even with few current firms?','If entry and exit are easy and sunk costs low, high prices invite rapid entry. Market concentration alone then overstates durable market power. The assumption is strongest where entrants can replicate incumbents quickly.'],
['Antitrust Economics','What is antitrust trying to preserve economically?','The target is competitive process and consumer or total welfare depending on the legal regime. Analysis focuses on market power, entry, innovation and exclusion rather than treating firm size itself as the offense.'],
['Merger Analysis','Why can a merger raise both efficiency and market power?','Combining firms may reduce duplicative fixed costs or create complementarities while also eliminating a competitor. Modern merger analysis weighs predicted price, quality and innovation effects against verifiable efficiencies and entry.'],
['Dynamic Competition','Why can innovation change how market power should be measured?','A firm can dominate current sales yet face a credible threat from technological replacement. Conversely, control over standards, data or acquisitions can block future competition. Static concentration is therefore only one piece of the analysis.']
]),
...g('Game Theory',[
['Strategic Form Game','What information is contained in a normal-form game?','Players, available strategies and payoffs for every strategy combination. It abstracts from the sequence of moves, making it useful for simultaneous or strategically equivalent interactions.'],
['Extensive Form Game','Why does the extensive form matter when timing changes incentives?','A game tree records who moves when, what they know and which actions are available. This allows analysis of credible threats, sequential rationality and information sets that a payoff matrix can hide.'],
['Dominant Strategy','Why is a dominant strategy unusually robust?','It yields at least as high a payoff regardless of what opponents do. The player therefore does not need beliefs about others’ strategies. Many economically important games do not possess one.'],
['Best Response','What does a best-response function reveal?','It maps each opponent strategy into the player’s payoff-maximizing response. Nash equilibria occur where best responses intersect, making reaction functions central to oligopoly and strategic analysis.'],
['Nash Equilibrium','What does Nash equilibrium guarantee?','Given everyone else’s strategy, no player benefits from unilateral deviation. It does not guarantee uniqueness, efficiency, fairness or stability under learning. It is a consistency concept for strategic plans.'],
['Mixed Strategy','Why would a rational player randomize deliberately?','Randomization can keep an opponent indifferent and prevent exploitation when no pure strategy is stable. In equilibrium the probabilities are chosen so each player is willing to mix among the strategies used.'],
['Subgame-Perfect Equilibrium','What problem does subgame perfection fix?','A Nash equilibrium can rely on threats that would be irrational to carry out once reached. Subgame perfection requires equilibrium play in every subgame, eliminating noncredible off-path threats in perfect-information settings.'],
['Backward Induction','Why can solving a game from the end change earlier behavior?','Players anticipate how later decision-makers will respond. Working backward identifies current actions that are optimal given those future responses, making commitment and timing economically valuable.'],
['Repeated Games','Why can repetition sustain cooperation that is impossible in a one-shot game?','Current defection can trigger future punishment, making short-run gains costly when players value the future. Reputation and monitoring therefore create incentives absent in a single interaction.'],
['Folk Theorem','Why can repeated interaction generate many equilibria rather than one prediction?','When players are patient and deviations can be punished, a wide range of feasible individually rational payoffs can be sustained. Repetition increases strategic possibilities but creates an equilibrium-selection problem.'],
['Credible Threat','What makes a threat credible?','The threatening action must be optimal or otherwise committed to if the relevant node is reached. Cheap verbal threats that would hurt the threatener ex post should not influence a rational opponent.'],
['Commitment','How can reducing your own future flexibility improve bargaining power?','An irreversible action can change the opponent’s best response by making certain future choices impossible. Capacity investment, contracts and delegation can therefore create strategic value through commitment.'],
['Sequential Equilibrium','What does sequential equilibrium add beyond subgame perfection?','It handles games with imperfect information by requiring strategies to be optimal given beliefs at every information set, with beliefs consistent with strategies through Bayes’ rule where possible.'],
['Bayesian Game','How are private types incorporated into game theory?','Players have beliefs about opponents’ private information such as cost, quality or valuation. Strategies map each type into an action, and equilibrium requires optimal behavior given beliefs about the distribution of types.'],
['Incomplete Information','Why can uncertainty about another player’s type change strategic behavior?','Actions can reveal information and beliefs affect best responses. Pricing, education, warranties and financial structure can therefore serve both allocative and signaling roles.'],
['Signaling Game','What makes a signal credible?','It must be sufficiently more attractive or less costly for one type than another so imitation is not profitable. Otherwise the action conveys no information in equilibrium.'],
['Screening Game','How does screening reverse the direction of information revelation?','The uninformed side designs a menu of contracts that induces informed types to self-select. Insurance deductibles and lending contracts are classic applications.'],
['Bargaining','What determines the division of surplus in bargaining models?','Outside options, patience, information and bargaining protocol all matter. Bargaining power is therefore an equilibrium consequence of alternatives and timing, not just personality.'],
['Nash Bargaining Solution','What axioms lead to the Nash bargaining solution?','Under efficiency, symmetry, invariance and independence conditions, the solution maximizes the product of players’ gains over disagreement payoffs. Stronger outside options shift the negotiated allocation.'],
['Coordination Game','Why can several equilibria make expectations self-fulfilling?','If each player wants to match what others do, more than one mutually consistent outcome can exist. History, focal points, communication or policy can determine which equilibrium is selected.'],
["Prisoner's Dilemma",'Why does individually dominant behavior generate a collectively inferior result?','Each player gains from defection regardless of the other’s action, yet mutual defection leaves both worse off than mutual cooperation. The game isolates the tension between private incentives and collective outcomes.'],
['Chicken','Why does commitment matter so much in a game of chicken?','Each player wants the other to yield but prefers yielding to mutual disaster. Convincingly removing your own ability to back down can force the opponent to do so, making strategic rigidity potentially advantageous.'],
['Battle of the Sexes','What does this game show about coordination with distributional conflict?','Both players prefer coordinating to separating, but disagree over which coordinated outcome. Communication can help, yet bargaining over who gets the preferred equilibrium remains central.'],
['Auction Theory','Why can the auction format change bids even for the same object?','Payment rules alter the strategic relationship between bids, information and the chance of winning. First-price, second-price and common-value auctions therefore induce different equilibrium bidding behavior.'],
["Winner's Curse",'Why should winning itself cause you to revise your estimate of value?','In a common-value auction, the winner tends to be the bidder with the most optimistic signal. Conditional on winning, your estimate is therefore selected upward. Rational bidders shade bids to account for this inference.'],
['Second-Price Auction','Why is truthful bidding a dominant strategy in a private-value second-price auction?','Your bid determines whether you win, but the payment is the highest competing bid rather than your own. Bidding your true value maximizes the set of cases in which winning is beneficial without increasing the price paid.'],
['First-Price Auction','Why do bidders shade below value in a first-price auction?','Winning at your full value leaves no surplus. The bidder trades off a higher probability of winning against a lower profit conditional on winning. Optimal shading depends on beliefs about rivals.'],
['All-Pay Auction','Why can lobbying resemble an all-pay auction?','Every contestant pays its effort cost regardless of whether it wins the prize. Competition can therefore dissipate a large share of the rent being contested, linking auction theory to rent seeking.']
]),
...g('Information, Contracts & Mechanism Design',[
['Asymmetric Information','Why can hidden information alter who participates in a market?','Prices affect not only quantity but the composition of buyers or sellers when types differ privately. Raising an insurance premium, for example, can disproportionately drive out low-risk customers and worsen the remaining pool.'],
['Adverse Selection','Why does adverse selection occur before contracting?','One side knows its type or risk before the agreement and participation depends on that private information. Contracts must therefore account for who selects into the market, not only behavior after signing.'],
['Moral Hazard','Why is moral hazard a post-contract problem?','Insurance or limited liability changes the marginal consequences of actions that are imperfectly observed. Contract terms such as deductibles, collateral or bonuses retain some exposure to behavior and therefore restore incentives.'],
['Principal–Agent Problem','Why does delegation create an economic problem even when both parties sign voluntarily?','The principal cannot perfectly observe the agent’s effort or information, and their objectives differ. Contracts trade off incentives, risk sharing and measurement error rather than simply commanding the desired action.'],
['Hidden Action','How does hidden action differ from hidden type?','Hidden action concerns behavior chosen after contracting, such as effort or risk taking. Hidden type exists before contracting. The first generates moral hazard; the second generates adverse selection.'],
['Hidden Information','Why can private information create rents for informed agents?','To induce truthful revelation, a contract may need to leave high-productivity or low-risk types extra surplus. These information rents are the price of satisfying incentive compatibility when types cannot be verified directly.'],
['Signaling','Why can education signal productivity even if it adds no skill?','If acquiring education is less costly for high-productivity workers, they may choose more of it to distinguish themselves. Employers infer type from the costly action. The signal is informative because imitation is expensive.'],
['Screening','Why are screening contracts intentionally distorted?','The uninformed party changes terms so types voluntarily separate. A high deductible can deter high-risk insurance customers from mimicking low-risk ones. The distortion preserves information revelation but sacrifices some efficiency.'],
['Incentive Compatibility','What must an incentive-compatible mechanism accomplish?','Each participant must prefer the action or report the designer wants from their true type over profitable misrepresentation. This constraint turns private information into a formal design problem.'],
['Participation Constraint','Why must a contract beat an outside option?','Even a perfectly incentive-compatible agreement fails if the agent prefers not to participate. Individual rationality therefore imposes a minimum utility or profit condition alongside incentive constraints.'],
['Information Rent','Why might an efficient type earn surplus even in a competitive contracting environment?','If its private type cannot be verified, extracting all surplus can make imitation incentives impossible to control. Leaving rent to the informed party can be necessary to elicit truthful behavior.'],
['Incomplete Contracts','Why do gaps in contracts create bargaining power later?','Not every future action can be specified or verified. When unforeseen states occur, ownership and outside options determine who controls adaptation, affecting investment incentives before the uncertainty is resolved.'],
['Hold-Up Problem','Why can efficient investment fail even when both parties know it would create value?','A relationship-specific investment weakens the investor’s outside option after it is sunk. Anticipating future renegotiation and rent capture, the investor rationally scales back today.'],
['Relational Contract','How can repeated interaction enforce promises that courts cannot verify?','Future business and reputation act as collateral. If the present gain from cheating is smaller than the discounted value of future cooperation, self-enforcing agreements can govern dimensions that formal contracts cannot.'],
['Reputation','Why can reputation substitute for direct monitoring?','Current behavior changes beliefs and therefore future trading opportunities. The threat of losing future rents can discipline quality or honesty even when a single transaction is hard to verify.'],
['Mechanism Design','How does mechanism design reverse the usual game-theory question?','Instead of taking the rules as given and predicting behavior, the designer chooses rules so strategic behavior produces a desired allocation. Incentive compatibility and information constraints determine what is implementable.'],
['Revelation Principle','Why can designers focus on mechanisms where agents report types truthfully?','Any outcome achievable by some equilibrium mechanism can, under broad conditions, be replicated by a direct mechanism in which truthful reporting is an equilibrium. The principle simplifies analysis without claiming people are naturally honest.'],
['Auction Design','What does an auction designer choose beyond who gets the object?','Information disclosure, reserve prices, payment rules, timing and eligibility all shape bidding incentives and revenue. Efficient allocation and revenue maximization need not coincide under every information structure.'],
['Matching Markets','Why do some allocation problems work poorly with ordinary prices?','Schools, residency positions or organ exchanges involve preferences on both sides, legal limits or nontransferable objectives. Matching mechanisms seek stable or strategy-resistant assignments without relying solely on money.'],
['Stable Matching','What does stability mean in a matching market?','There is no unmatched pair that would both prefer to be matched with each other over their assigned partners. Stability prevents arrangements from unraveling through mutually attractive deviations.'],
['Deferred Acceptance','Why is the Gale–Shapley deferred-acceptance algorithm important?','It produces stable matches and, for the proposing side, makes truthful preference reporting strategically attractive under standard assumptions. The algorithm transformed market design into a practical engineering field.'],
['Market Design','What makes market design different from ordinary policy analysis?','Rules, timing and information are treated as design variables. Economists use game theory and empirical evidence to build auctions, matching systems and exchanges whose equilibrium behavior better meets allocation objectives.'],
['Contract Theory','What unifies agency, screening and incomplete-contract models?','All study how agreements allocate risk, information and control when behavior or states cannot be perfectly observed and enforced. The optimal contract is constrained by what can be measured, verified and credibly committed to.'],
['Multitasking Problem','Why can stronger incentives on one measured task reduce overall performance?','Agents shift effort toward rewarded dimensions and away from unmeasured ones. High-powered incentives can therefore distort teaching, medicine or management when performance is multidimensional.'],
['Career Concerns','How can future reputation motivate effort even without an explicit bonus?','Current performance affects beliefs about ability and therefore future wages or assignments. Young workers may exert extra effort to influence those beliefs, while older workers with established reputations face weaker career incentives.'],
['Efficiency Wages','How can paying above the market-clearing wage solve an information problem?','Higher pay makes job loss more costly, potentially increasing effort when monitoring is imperfect. It can also reduce turnover and improve applicant quality. The result can support involuntary unemployment in equilibrium.'],
['Collateral','Why does collateral change both selection and incentives in lending?','Borrowers who pledge valuable assets bear more downside and may be safer types, while the threat of loss discourages risky behavior. Collateral therefore addresses adverse selection and moral hazard simultaneously, though it can exclude productive but asset-poor borrowers.']
]),
...g('Externalities & Public Goods',[
['Externality','What makes an effect an externality rather than a normal market interaction?','It changes another party’s utility or production possibilities through a channel not priced in the decision-maker’s marginal incentives. The gap between private and social marginal effects creates the inefficiency.'],
['Negative Externality','Why does an unpriced negative externality lead to excessive activity?','The decision-maker considers private cost but not the harm imposed on others. Private marginal cost lies below social marginal cost, so the decentralized quantity exceeds the efficient level.'],
['Positive Externality','Why can beneficial spillovers lead to underinvestment?','The investor captures only part of the benefit created for others. Education, research and vaccination can therefore have social returns above private returns, reducing private provision relative to the efficient level.'],
['Pigouvian Tax','Why should a corrective tax equal marginal rather than average damage?','The decision being corrected concerns one additional unit. Setting the tax equal to marginal external harm aligns the private cost of that unit with its social cost at the efficient quantity.'],
['Pigouvian Subsidy','When is a subsidy the mirror image of a Pigouvian tax?','If an activity creates a positive marginal external benefit, subsidizing it by that amount can align private and social incentives. Measurement and fiscal costs determine how well the theoretical rule can be implemented.'],
['Coase Theorem','What does the Coase theorem actually say about bargaining?','With clearly defined rights, negligible transaction costs and complete bargaining, parties can negotiate toward an efficient allocation regardless of the initial assignment of rights. The distribution of surplus still depends on who initially holds the right.'],
['Transaction Costs and Coase','Why do transaction costs determine whether bargaining can internalize an externality?','When many parties, measurement problems or enforcement costs make negotiation expensive, mutually beneficial deals can fail. The assignment of rights and public policy then affect efficiency, not only distribution.'],
['Property Rights','Why can defining property rights reduce overuse of a resource?','When users bear the opportunity cost of depletion and can exclude others, they have stronger incentives to conserve and invest. But defining and enforcing rights can itself be costly or politically difficult.'],
['Public Goods','Why does nonrivalry change how willingness to pay should be aggregated?','Everyone can consume the same unit, so efficient provision compares the cost of one unit with the sum of individual marginal benefits. Private markets struggle because users can benefit without paying when exclusion is difficult.'],
['Nonrivalry','Why can marginal cost of an additional user be near zero?','One person’s consumption does not materially reduce what remains for others. Digital information and national defense approximate this property, though congestion or capacity can make rivalry appear at scale.'],
['Nonexcludability','Why does difficulty excluding nonpayers create a financing problem?','Individuals can free ride on provision financed by others, understating willingness to pay. The result can be underprovision even when aggregate benefits exceed cost.'],
['Free-Rider Problem','Why can individually rational noncontribution destroy a collectively valuable project?','Each person hopes others will pay while still receiving the benefit. When everyone reasons similarly, funding collapses. Repetition, norms, selective incentives or taxation can solve the coordination problem.'],
['Common-Pool Resource','Why are common resources different from pure public goods?','They are difficult to exclude users from but rival in consumption: one person’s extraction leaves less for others. This creates depletion incentives absent from nonrival public goods.'],
['Tragedy of the Commons','Why does open access drive extraction beyond the efficient level?','Each user captures the full private benefit of an extra unit but bears only part of the depletion cost shared across all users. The wedge encourages excessive effort or harvest.'],
['Ostrom and Commons Governance','Why is privatization or central regulation not the only solution to common-resource problems?','Communities can develop monitoring, graduated sanctions, boundaries and collective rules that align extraction incentives. Ostrom’s work shows that local governance can succeed when users can communicate and enforce rules credibly.'],
['Club Goods','What makes a club good distinct from a public good?','Exclusion is feasible while use remains largely nonrival below congestion. Membership fees can therefore finance provision. Gyms, subscription platforms and toll roads often have club-good features.'],
['Congestion','How does congestion turn a nonrival service into a rival one at the margin?','An additional user imposes delay or crowding on existing users once capacity becomes scarce. Congestion pricing charges for that marginal external cost and can improve allocation without expanding capacity immediately.'],
['Carbon Pricing','Why is carbon pricing an externality policy rather than merely a revenue measure?','Emissions impose climate damages not reflected in ordinary fossil-fuel prices. A carbon tax or permit price makes emitters face a marginal cost tied to that external harm, encouraging abatement wherever it is cheapest.'],
['Cap-and-Trade','How does cap-and-trade separate the quantity target from the allocation of abatement?','The regulator fixes total permits while trading determines a common marginal emissions price. Firms with low abatement costs cut more and sell permits; high-cost firms buy, equalizing marginal abatement costs.'],
['Carbon Tax vs Cap','How does uncertainty affect the choice between a carbon tax and an emissions cap?','A tax fixes the marginal price while quantity adjusts; a cap fixes quantity while permit price adjusts. Relative slopes of marginal damage and abatement-cost curves determine which uncertainty is more costly.'],
['Tradable Permits','Why can trading rights achieve a fixed environmental target at lower cost?','Firms differ in abatement cost. Trading reallocates permits toward high-cost reducers while low-cost reducers cut more, equalizing marginal costs across sources if monitoring and markets work well.'],
['Externality Measurement','Why is average damage not enough for corrective policy?','Efficient decisions depend on the harm from one additional unit at the relevant quantity, location and time. Marginal damages can vary widely across sources, making uniform policy simpler but less precise.'],
['Double Dividend','Can an environmental tax improve efficiency twice?','Revenue can finance reductions in other distortionary taxes, creating a potential second benefit beyond correcting the externality. But interactions with existing taxes mean the second dividend is not automatic.'],
['Public-Good Provision','Why can government provision still fail even when the market underprovides?','Governments also need information about benefits, face political incentives and must raise revenue through distortionary taxes. Public-good theory identifies a market failure, not a guarantee of flawless public provision.'],
['Samuelson Condition','What is the efficient provision condition for a pure public good?','The sum of individuals’ marginal rates of substitution between the public good and private consumption should equal the marginal rate of transformation in production. Benefits add vertically because everyone consumes the same public quantity.'],
['Lindahl Pricing','How would personalized prices theoretically finance a public good?','Each individual pays a personalized tax share equal to their marginal valuation, and shares sum to the provision cost. The challenge is that people have incentives to understate valuations when those preferences are private.'],
['Environmental Externality','Why can location matter as much as total emissions?','Local pollutants cause damages that depend on population exposure, weather and ecosystem sensitivity. A uniform emissions price is therefore less precise for local pollution than for a globally mixed pollutant such as carbon dioxide.'],
['Knowledge Spillover','Why is knowledge a classic positive externality?','Ideas can often be copied or built upon without compensating the original creator fully. This raises the social return to research above the private return and motivates patents, subsidies and public research.'],
['Network Externality','When does one user’s adoption change another user’s payoff?','Compatibility, communication or complementary products make the value of joining depend on the installed base. The effect can generate tipping and coordination problems, though some benefits may already be internalized by platform pricing.']
])
];

